  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures through Friday, then \nseasonable with increasing clouds this weekend. \n\nA couple of consecutive days of light onshore flow has increased\nlow-level moisture enough to result in (1) river valley fog west\nof the Blue Ridge (locally dense at times), and (2) low clouds\nbanking along and east of the Blue Ridge and Catoctin Mountains.\nClouds/fog are more expansive relative to Wednesday morning, but\nshould burn off and lift by midday to early afternoon as winds\nshift from southeast to southwest.\n\nThe NAM seems to be handling the low-level moisture profiles\nbest, with low clouds favored east and areas of fog west (as\nopposed to a lot of other guidance which maintains a dense fog\nthreat to the east with minimal fog to the west).\n\nAfter the clouds break, mixing on these light southwest winds\nshould allow temperatures to reach into the lower to middle 70s\n(60s mountains). Low temperatures tonight will be seasonable\nin the 40s to lower 50s, but may be rather variable over short \ndistances depending on where goes calm and where holds onto a \nlight southwest wind. Higher elevations will actually hold on to\nmore southwest breezes and be poking up into the warming airmass,\nand could therefore be some of the warmest spots tonight.\n\nWell above normal temperatures persist Friday, despite a cold\nfront crossing the region. Cold air advection lags behind this\nfront, and downsloping flow likely allows for highs to push \nwell into the 70s (60s mountains). One caveat is if some \nstratocumulus develops on northwest flow, but the background \nairmass is rather dry so the chance of that happening and it \nhaving a significant impact on temperatures is limited.\n\nEventually, some cooler temperatures will filter into the region\nFriday night through the weekend. Expect seasonable and dry\nconditions as high pressure holds firm, with any precipitation\nbecoming less likely through at least Sunday evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances remain uncertain for the first \nhalf of next week. \n\nStrong high pressure will remain overhead Sunday and only gradually \ndrift eastward through the first half of next week. The Mid-Atlantic\nwill be caught in between more prominent but slow moving troughs\n(or closed lows) near Florida/the Bahamas, the Plains, and eastern\nCanada. Some models still show potential for a shortwave trough\nto ride around the departing ridge, but at this point the chance\nfor rain has dropped to 30 percent or less between Sunday night\nand Tuesday night. Even though conditions may largely remain dry,\nexpect more cloud cover between onshore surface flow and a warm\nadvection pattern aloft. Temperatures will likely remain a little\nabove normal through this period despite the extra clouds. The \nPlains trough may eventually increase rain chances Wednesday or \nThursday as it moves eastward, but its progression is uncertain \nas well.",
